"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[34917],{734917:e=>{e.exports="# Temporary Tables & CTEs\n\nOracle temporary table patterns → Delta temporary views, CTEs, and staging tables.\n\n> For DBSQL temporary table syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/temp-table.md`.\n\n## ORACLE\n\n### Global Temporary Tables (GTT)\n\nOracle's `CREATE GLOBAL TEMPORARY TABLE` defines a **persistent schema** whose **data is session- or transaction-scoped**. The table definition is shared across sessions; each session sees only its own rows.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle: schema is global, data is per-session\nCREATE GLOBAL TEMPORARY TABLE staging_orders (\n    order_id NUMBER,\n    status   VARCHAR2(20)\n) ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS;  -- or ON COMMIT DELETE ROWS for per-transaction\n```\n\n**Critical limitations of DBSQL TEMP TABLE:**\n\n1. **SQL warehouse sessions only** — Not supported in Notebook or Jobs contexts running on classic or serverless compute clusters; only available inside a SQL warehouse session\n2. **No `ALTER TABLE`** — Cannot add/drop columns after creation\n3. **Session-scoped** — Dropped automatically when the session ends\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL (SQL warehouse session)\nCREATE TEMP TABLE staging_orders (order_id INT, status STRING);\n```\n\n**`ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS` vs `ON COMMIT DELETE ROWS`:**\n\n- `ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS` — rows persist for the session. Closest DBSQL match is `CREATE TEMP TABLE`.\n- `ON COMMIT DELETE ROWS` — rows are deleted at each commit. DBSQL has no per-transaction truncation. Workarounds: `TRUNCATE` explicitly at the appropriate points, or rewrite to avoid the reliance on commit-boundary state.\n\n**Preferred alternatives (all contexts):**\n\n```sql\n-- CTEs (best for single-query scope)\nWITH temp_data AS (SELECT * FROM source WHERE condition)\nSELECT * FROM temp_data;\n\n-- Temporary views (session-scoped, works on notebooks AND serverless)\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW temp_data AS SELECT * FROM source WHERE condition;\n\n-- Permanent staging tables in Unity Catalog (most flexible for multi-step ETL)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.staging.temp_data AS SELECT ...;\n-- ... use ...\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS my_catalog.staging.temp_data;\n```\n\n### Private Temporary Tables (18c+)\n\nOracle 18c introduced `CREATE PRIVATE TEMPORARY TABLE ora$ptt_...` — table metadata is session-scoped (not just the data). Map the same way as GTTs to DBSQL `TEMP TABLE` (which is also session-scoped in metadata) or a temporary view.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle 18c+\nCREATE PRIVATE TEMPORARY TABLE ora$ptt_staging (id NUMBER, val VARCHAR2(50));\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE TEMP TABLE staging (id INT, val STRING);\n-- or\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW staging AS SELECT ... FROM ...;\n```\n\nPrivate temporary table names in Oracle are required to start with the `private_temp_table_prefix` (default `ora$ptt_`) — strip the prefix during migration.\n\n### `INSERT ... SELECT` into a GTT → temp view\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nCREATE GLOBAL TEMPORARY TABLE customer_totals (customer_id NUMBER, total NUMBER) ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS;\nINSERT INTO customer_totals\nSELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id;\n\n-- DBSQL (temp view approach — works on all contexts)\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW customer_totals AS\nSELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders\nGROUP BY customer_id;\n```\n\n### Cross-session shared temporary state\n\nOracle GTTs are **not** cross-session — each session sees its own rows, and Oracle has no feature equivalent to a \"global cross-session temp table\". If your source uses a regular table as a cross-session scratch area, use a permanent staging table in Unity Catalog with a unique name and `DROP` it after use.\n\n**Naming strategy to avoid collisions under concurrent pipeline runs**: include a run identifier in the table name so parallel executions don't clobber each other.\n\n```sql\n-- Naming pattern: include a run_id or timestamp suffix\n-- In a Databricks Workflow, use the job run ID: {{ job.run_id }} (available as a task parameter)\n\n-- Create with unique name\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.staging.cross_session_scratch_run_12345 AS\nSELECT * FROM source WHERE condition;\n\n-- ... use across multiple sessions/tasks ...\n\n-- Drop when done\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS my_catalog.staging.cross_session_scratch_run_12345;\n```\n\nIf a run ID is not available, use a UUID generated before the pipeline starts and passed as a parameter to all tasks that share the staging table.\n\n### PL/SQL collections and record types\n\nOracle PL/SQL has in-memory collection types:\n\n- `TABLE OF <type> INDEX BY BINARY_INTEGER` (associative array)\n- `TABLE OF <type>` (nested table, with `BULK COLLECT`)\n- `VARRAY(n) OF <type>` (bounded array)\n\nThese live entirely in PL/SQL memory — not SQL objects — and have **no direct DBSQL equivalent**. The right replacement depends on usage scope:\n\n**Single-query scope** (the collection is populated once and read once): replace with a CTE.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle (PL/SQL collection used in one query)\nDECLARE\n    TYPE t_ids IS TABLE OF NUMBER;\n    v_ids t_ids;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT id BULK COLLECT INTO v_ids FROM source WHERE active = 1;\n    FOR i IN 1..v_ids.COUNT LOOP\n        UPDATE other SET flag = 1 WHERE id = v_ids(i);\n    END LOOP;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL (set-based rewrite — preferred)\nUPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.other\nSET flag = 1\nWHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = 1);\n\n-- DBSQL (CTE rewrite, if update needs joining on more columns)\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.other t\nUSING (SELECT id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = 1) s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET flag = 1;\n```\n\n**Multi-statement scope** (the collection is populated once and queried across several separate statements): replace with a temporary view. A CTE does not work here because it is only visible inside the single statement that defines it.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle multi-statement pattern\nDECLARE\n    TYPE t_ids IS TABLE OF NUMBER;\n    v_ids t_ids;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT id BULK COLLECT INTO v_ids FROM source WHERE active = 1;\n    UPDATE other SET flag = 1 WHERE id IN (SELECT COLUMN_VALUE FROM TABLE(v_ids));\n    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v_cnt FROM TABLE(v_ids);\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL: temp view (session-scoped, visible across multiple statements)\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW t AS SELECT id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = 1;\nUPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.other SET flag = 1 WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM t);\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM t;\n```\n\n**Collection-type variants — pick the right rewrite:**\n\n| Oracle collection type                                                  | Population pattern                              | DBSQL replacement                                                                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `TABLE OF <t> INDEX BY BINARY_INTEGER` (associative array, integer key) | `arr(i) := value;`                              | If used as an ordered list → CTE / temp view. If used as a counter / accumulator → individual variables or refactor to set-based SQL. |\n| `TABLE OF <t> INDEX BY VARCHAR2(...)` (associative array, string key)   | `arr('key') := value;`                          | DBSQL has no string-keyed in-memory map. Stage as `MAP<STRING, ...>` literal in a CTE, or use a 2-column temp view (key, value).      |\n| `TABLE OF <t>` (nested table, no INDEX BY)                              | `BULK COLLECT INTO`, `EXTEND`, indexing by 1..N | CTE / temp view (the dense-int case is functionally identical to an associative-int array after migration).                           |\n| `VARRAY(n) OF <t>`                                                      | Bounded ordered array, often passed to objects  | CTE / temp view. The bound `n` is dropped — DBSQL has no element-count cap on tables.                                                 |\n| `TABLE OF rec_t` (collection of records)                                | `BULK COLLECT INTO rec_tab`                     | CTE / temp view with one column per record field. Drop the record type entirely.                                                      |\n| `TABLE OF TABLE OF <t>` (nested-of-nested, multidim)                    | Rare, usually domain modelling                  | TODO — flatten to a single 2-D table with extra key columns. No DBSQL equivalent for native multidim collections.                     |\n\n**Collection methods (`.COUNT`, `.FIRST`, `.LAST`, `.NEXT`, `.PRIOR`, `.EXISTS`, `.EXTEND`, `.DELETE`, `.TRIM`, `.LIMIT`).** Oracle exposes a rich method API on every collection. None of them have a direct DBSQL equivalent — when you migrate to a CTE / temp view, the methods disappear and you operate on the underlying SQL instead:\n\n| Oracle method                            | Used to express                           | DBSQL replacement                                                                                   |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `v_arr.COUNT`                            | Number of populated elements              | `(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM v_arr_view)` — runs against the migrated view/CTE                            |\n| `v_arr.FIRST` / `v_arr.LAST`             | Lowest / highest populated index          | `(SELECT MIN(idx) FROM v_arr_view)` / `MAX(idx)` — only meaningful if you preserved an `idx` column |\n| `v_arr.NEXT(i)` / `v_arr.PRIOR(i)`       | Next / previous populated index after `i` | `(SELECT MIN(idx) FROM v_arr_view WHERE idx > i)` / `MAX(idx) WHERE idx < i`                        |\n| `v_arr.EXISTS(i)`                        | Is index `i` populated?                   | `(SELECT 1 FROM v_arr_view WHERE idx = i)` returns NULL if absent                                   |\n| `v_arr.EXTEND(n)`                        | Grow nested table / VARRAY by `n` slots   | Not needed — DBSQL views/CTEs grow implicitly via INSERT/UNION ALL                                  |\n| `v_arr.DELETE(i)` / `v_arr.DELETE(i, j)` | Remove element(s)                         | `DELETE FROM v_arr_temp WHERE idx = i` (only if backed by a real / temp table) — CTEs are immutable |\n| `v_arr.DELETE` (no arg)                  | Empty the collection                      | `TRUNCATE TABLE v_arr_temp` (table-backed); recreate the temp view with an empty SELECT             |\n| `v_arr.TRIM(n)`                          | Remove last `n` elements                  | Not migratable — refactor to a bounded SELECT                                                       |\n| `v_arr.LIMIT`                            | Declared maximum size of a VARRAY         | DBSQL views / CTEs are unbounded — drop the check                                                   |\n| `v_arr := v_other`                       | Whole-collection assignment               | `CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW v_arr_view AS SELECT * FROM v_other_view`                         |\n| `v_arr = v_other` / `v_arr <> v_other`   | Whole-collection comparison               | `(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ((SELECT * FROM a) MINUS (SELECT * FROM b))) = 0` semantics — flag with TODO |\n| `v_arr IS NULL`                          | Uninitialized nested table / VARRAY       | TODO — DBSQL has no \"uninitialized\" state; views / temp tables either exist or don't                |\n\nIf the original code drives control flow with `.COUNT > 0`, `.FIRST .. .LAST`, or `.EXISTS(i)`, rewrite the surrounding loop as a single set-based SQL statement first — set-based equivalents almost always eliminate the need for these methods.\n\n**MULTISET operators.** When Oracle code combines two nested-table collections with `MULTISET UNION`, `MULTISET INTERSECT`, `MULTISET EXCEPT`, `MEMBER OF`, `SUBMULTISET OF`, `IS A SET`, or `IS [NOT] EMPTY`, translate to set operators on the migrated views / CTEs:\n\n| Oracle                   | DBSQL                                                                                |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `a MULTISET UNION b`     | `SELECT * FROM a UNION ALL SELECT * FROM b` (drop ALL for `MULTISET UNION DISTINCT`) |\n| `a MULTISET INTERSECT b` | `SELECT * FROM a INTERSECT SELECT * FROM b`                                          |\n| `a MULTISET EXCEPT b`    | `SELECT * FROM a EXCEPT SELECT * FROM b`                                             |\n| `value MEMBER OF a`      | `EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM a WHERE col = value)` (or `value IN (SELECT col FROM a)`)     |\n| `a SUBMULTISET OF b`     | `(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ((SELECT * FROM a) EXCEPT (SELECT * FROM b))) = 0`            |\n| `a IS [NOT] EMPTY`       | `(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM a) [>] 0`                                                     |\n| `a IS A SET`             | `(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM a) = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT *) FROM a)` — flag, rare          |\n| `SET(a)`                 | `SELECT DISTINCT * FROM a`                                                           |\n| `CARDINALITY(a)`         | `(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM a)` — see `10-function-catalog.md` \xa7Collection sizing         |\n\n**`BULK COLLECT INTO` — context-dependent rewrite:**\n\n`BULK COLLECT INTO` is just the population step for a collection. The rewrite depends on what the collection is _used for_ afterwards, so always look at the surrounding code:\n\n| Surrounding pattern                                                | Rewrite                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Bulk-collect, then drive a `FORALL INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE`           | Single set-based DML — drop both the BULK COLLECT and the FORALL. See FORALL section below.                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Bulk-collect, then iterate with `FOR i IN 1..v.COUNT LOOP ... END` | Set-based DML, or a single `FOR rec AS SELECT ... DO` cursor loop. Drop the collection variable entirely.                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| Bulk-collect, then return as a `SYS_REFCURSOR` to the caller       | Final `SELECT` in the procedure body — the result is returned implicitly. See `04-procedures-variables.md` \xa7REF CURSOR.                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| Bulk-collect with `LIMIT n`, paginated processing                  | If the loop body is set-based, drop pagination entirely (DBSQL processes the whole set in one shot). If the loop body genuinely needs row-by-row work, fall back to a cursor with a `LIMIT n` page in the SELECT and an outer offset variable — flag with a TODO since pagination semantics rarely survive intact. |\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle: BULK COLLECT + FORALL (typical \"ETL in PL/SQL\" shape)\nDECLARE\n    TYPE t_rows IS TABLE OF source%ROWTYPE;\n    v_rows t_rows;\n    CURSOR c IS SELECT id, val FROM source WHERE active = 1;\nBEGIN\n    OPEN c;\n    LOOP\n        FETCH c BULK COLLECT INTO v_rows LIMIT 1000;\n        EXIT WHEN v_rows.COUNT = 0;\n        FORALL i IN 1..v_rows.COUNT\n            INSERT INTO target (id, val) VALUES (v_rows(i).id, v_rows(i).val);\n    END LOOP;\n    CLOSE c;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL — drop the entire bulk machinery; this is one INSERT\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.target (id, val)\nSELECT id, val FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = 1;\n```\n\n**`FORALL` — bulk DML.** `FORALL i IN 1..v.COUNT INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE ...` runs one DML per array element with shared parse / context-switch optimization. **DBSQL has no FORALL** — every real-world FORALL collapses to a single set-based DML against the source query that populated the array:\n\n| Oracle FORALL pattern                                                    | DBSQL rewrite                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `FORALL i IN 1..v.COUNT INSERT INTO t VALUES (v(i).a, v(i).b);`          | `INSERT INTO t SELECT a, b FROM <source query that populated v>;`                                                                                                                            |\n| `FORALL i IN 1..v.COUNT UPDATE t SET col = v(i).col WHERE id = v(i).id;` | `MERGE INTO t USING <source query> s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET col = s.col;`                                                                                               |\n| `FORALL i IN 1..v.COUNT DELETE FROM t WHERE id = v(i).id;`               | `DELETE FROM t WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM <source query>)`                                                                                                                                  |\n| `FORALL ... SAVE EXCEPTIONS`                                             | No equivalent. Either drop the row-level continue-on-error semantics, or pre-validate the source query and stage rejects in a side table — see `02-select-dml.md` \xa7`LOG ERRORS`.             |\n| `FORALL ... INDICES OF v` / `VALUES OF v` (sparse arrays)                | Inherent in set-based DML — sparse rows simply aren't in the source query.                                                                                                                   |\n| `SQL%BULK_ROWCOUNT(i)` / `SQL%BULK_EXCEPTIONS`                           | No equivalent. Single-statement DBSQL DML reports a single `ROW_COUNT` via `GET DIAGNOSTICS`.                                                                                                |\n| `RETURNING ... BULK COLLECT INTO`                                        | DBSQL has no `RETURNING` on `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE`. Re-query the target table by primary key after the DML, or use Delta CDF to capture changes. See `02-select-dml.md` \xa7RETURNING INTO. |\n\nIf you cannot trace the FORALL back to a single source query (e.g. the array was hand-populated element-by-element across branching control flow), flag the procedure with a TODO and reconstruct the source query from the population logic.\n\n### Records and `%ROWTYPE`\n\nOracle PL/SQL records are composite types declared with `TYPE rec_t IS RECORD (a NUMBER, b VARCHAR2(50))` or implicitly via `tab%ROWTYPE`. They have **no direct DBSQL equivalent**. Three rewrite paths depending on how the record is used:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    TYPE order_rec_t IS RECORD (id NUMBER, amount NUMBER, status VARCHAR2(20));\n    v_order order_rec_t;\n    v_row   orders%ROWTYPE;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT id, amount, status INTO v_order FROM orders WHERE id = 42;\n    v_row.status := 'done';\n    INSERT INTO orders_archive VALUES v_row;\nEND;\n/\n```\n\n| Usage shape                                           | DBSQL replacement                                                                                               |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Local scratch variable used to ferry a few fields     | Refactor to individual `DECLARE`d scalars — drop the record type entirely. **Most common case.**                |\n| Returned from a function / passed as parameter        | Use a `STRUCT<a:INT, b:STRING>` typed variable, or return / accept individual columns.                          |\n| `INSERT INTO t VALUES <record>` (whole-record INSERT) | Spell out the column list: `INSERT INTO t (a, b, c) VALUES (v.a, v.b, v.c)` — DBSQL needs the explicit columns. |\n| `tab%ROWTYPE` declaration                             | Resolve to the concrete column types at migration time (DBSQL has no `%ROWTYPE`).                               |\n| Collection of records (`TABLE OF rec_t`)              | Replace with a multi-column CTE / temp view — see the collection table above.                                   |\n\n### `TABLE()` function on collections — no DBSQL equivalent\n\nOracle allows querying a collection like a table: `SELECT * FROM TABLE(v_collection)`. DBSQL has no such feature. Collections must be materialized into a real table, temp view, or CTE first.\n\n### Table-Valued Parameters — no DBSQL equivalent\n\nNo DBSQL equivalent to passing a table into a procedure. Replace with:\n\n- Temporary staging table populated before the call\n- Serialized array parameter passed as JSON string, parsed inside the procedure with `from_json`\n- Delta Live Tables pipeline if the call is part of an ETL flow\n\n### `WITH` (subquery factoring) — identical\n\nOracle CTEs (`WITH x AS (...) SELECT ...`) translate one-to-one to DBSQL CTEs. Recursive CTEs translate directly — see `02-select-dml.md CONNECT BY` section for Oracle hierarchical queries.\n"}}]);
